From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Murray <address-in-upstream-commit@localhost>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 21:21:42 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 87/93] arm64: dts: apple: t6050: expose the K boards' jack
 pins with the PCIe PERST# pin

The K board headphone jack change opens pins 134 and 139 (the CS42L84's
interrupt and reset) in the shared K114/K116 pinctrl_ap list, and this branch
opens pin 80 for PCIe port 0. Use the union, <0 80>, <81 53>, <135 4>,
<140 81>, in both so they land in either order. Both pins read cleanly; only
pin 112 takes an SError.

Signed-off-by: Ryan Murray <address-in-upstream-commit@localhost>

[re-cut onto the K114s r3d tree]
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t6050-k114-k116.dtsi | 6 +++---
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t6050-k114-k116.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t6050-k114-k116.dtsi
index 757df032c13..d5ff788f8a6 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t6050-k114-k116.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t6050-k114-k116.dtsi
@@ -120,11 +120,11 @@ &i2c2 {
 
 /*
  * As on J716c, where reading pin 112 takes an SError, only the pins a node
- * names are exposed: here 80, PCIe port 0's PERST#. A board that names more
- * pins replaces the list.
+ * names are exposed: 80, PCIe port 0's PERST#, and 134 and 139, the jack
+ * codec's interrupt and reset. A board that names more pins replaces the list.
  */
 &pinctrl_ap {
-	gpio-reserved-ranges = <0 80>, <81 140>;
+	gpio-reserved-ranges = <0 80>, <81 53>, <135 4>, <140 81>;
 };
 
